<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Deuteronomy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deuteronomy is the fifth book of the Torah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, presented as the farewell speeches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> before they enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>. It insists on the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> alone at a single central sanctuary and sets out a code of law. Many scholars identify an early form of it with the book of the law found in the temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> around 622 BCE, which inspired King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>'s religious reform.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy.jpg" alt="Book of Deuteronomy" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pap_266.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
